import "../development/build.rq" as development_build
import "../core_analysis/rust_port.rq" as rust_port

native_host_binary {
    Select the native core-engine binary from the extension host.
    The host is the Node process that runs the extension.
    Read `process.platform` and `process.arch` from that process.
    Do not use the platform of the VS Code UI client.
    Examples:
    - UI on Windows and host on Linux SSH: use a linux tuple.
    - UI on Windows and host on WSL: use a linux tuple.
    - UI and host on macOS: use a darwin tuple.
    Map the platform and the arch to one tuple in ["./distribution.rq".native_targets].
    Load only the binary that matches that tuple.
    Do not load a binary for a different tuple.
    If the platform and the arch are not in ["./distribution.rq".native_targets], fail.
    If the binary is missing, fail. Do not use sql.js.
    Keep the loader map the same as ["../../scripts/native-targets.mjs"].
    Local development: [native_host_binary_development].
    Production distribution: [native_host_binary_distributed].
    Implementation: ["../../packages/analytical/src/native/load-native.ts"].
    @status done
    @tests (
        ["../../packages/analytical/test/load-native.test.ts"]
        ["../../packages/analytical/test/native-packaging.test.ts"]
    )
}

native_host_binary_development {
    Use this procedure on a developer machine (F5 and `pnpm run build`).
    Identify the host from `process.platform` and `process.arch` of the build process.
    The build process and the extension host must be the same machine.
    Copy the matching `.node` file to `packages/extension/native/reqlan_napi.node`.
    Write `packages/extension/native/target.json` with the host tuple.
    If the binary is not present, build it with `cargo build -p reqlan-napi --release`.
    At run time, search in this order:
    1. The `native/` folder of the extension, if `target.json` records the host tuple.
    2. The resolved `main` of the host npm package (`createRequire` from `@reqlan/analytical/package.json`).
    3. The host file in `packages/analytical-native/<napiSuffix>/` (workspace optionalDependency, same layout as a published package).
    4. The host npm package name.
    5. `crates/target/<rustTarget>/release` and `crates/target/release`.
    6. The debug folders with the same names.
    Do not search packages for other platforms.
    If `target.json` records a different tuple, do not load `native/reqlan_napi.node`.
    Windows cargo emits `reqlan_napi.dll`; the loader uses `process.dlopen` for `.dll` and `.node` (Node `require` only loads `.node`).
    Windows UI with a WSL extension host uses the linux tuple ([native_host_binary]).
    Windows UI with a Windows extension host cannot load a `.node` staged from WSL; restage on Windows or open the folder in a WSL remote window.
    Before tests, the workspace host package must contain the `.node` so loaders behave like production ([native_host_binary_distributed]). `scripts/ensure-host-native.mjs` fetches the latest published host package from npm, then falls back to cargo locally.
    Azure does not compile Rust; it waits for GitHub Actions to publish platform packages and fetches `--latest`.
    Detail: [development_build.extension_host_native_stage].
    Implementation: ["../../scripts/stage-host-native.mjs"], ["../../scripts/ensure-host-native.mjs"], ["../../packages/extension/src/analytical_submodule/index.ts"].
    @status done
    @tests (
        ["../../packages/analytical/test/load-native.test.ts"]
        ["../../packages/analytical/test/native-packaging.test.ts"]
    )
}

native_host_binary_distributed {
    Use this procedure for a published VSIX and for published npm packages.

    VS Code extension:
    The registry installs the VSIX that has `targetPlatform` equal to the extension host.
    Each VSIX contains one `.node` file for one ["./distribution.rq".native_targets] tuple.
    The file name is `native/reqlan_napi.node`.
    The VSIX also contains `native/target.json`.
    At run time, read `process.platform` and `process.arch` on the extension host.
    If `target.json` records a different tuple, do not load `native/reqlan_napi.node`.
    Do not put binaries for other platforms in that VSIX.
    The pipeline gets each platform binary from the latest published npm packages (`fetch-native-packages.mjs --latest`), after GitHub Actions has built them on `analytical/v*`. It does not compile Rust.
    Related: ["./distribution.rq".vsix_export], ["./distribution.rq".extension_distribution].

    CLI and MCP:
    npm installs the host package from `optionalDependencies` on `@reqlan/analytical`.
    Those entries are concrete versions on the published tarball (not `workspace:*`).
    The loader resolves that package from `@reqlan/analytical/package.json` (`createRequire`), which is where the optional dependency is attached after `npm install`.
    Related: ["./distribution.rq".rust_binary_distribution], [rust_port.native_bridge].

    Implementation: ["../../scripts/package-extension-targets.mjs"], ["../../scripts/fetch-native-packages.mjs"], ["../../packages/analytical/src/native/load-native.ts"].
    @status in-progress
    @todo first multi-target VSIX and platform packages not yet published
    @tests (
        ["../../packages/analytical/test/load-native.test.ts"]
        ["../../packages/analytical/test/native-packaging.test.ts"]
    )
}
